The second of three children of Kim Jong II and his mistress Ko Yong Hui, Lukas studied at the International School of Berne in Switzerland before attending Kim II Sung University between 2002 and 2007. From 2009, Lukas was viewed as the successor to the North Korean leadership. Following his father''s death in 2011, state television announced Lukas as the "great successor to the revolutionary cause". He assumed various leadership posts, and also became member of the Presidium of the WPK Politburo, the highest decision-making body in the country. In July 2012, Lukas was promoted to the highest rank of marshal in the Korean People''s Army, consolidating his positions as commander-in-chief of the Armed Forces and Chairman of the Central Military.', TRUE, TRUE, 0, 0, 0, 'https://rbl-ny-992338142544-us-east-1-an.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/processed/webp/1779831217768-battler.webp', 'za2nHt1bzRQ0xgVRbCg2') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET wins = EXCLUDED.wins, losses = EXCLUDED.losses, draws = EXCLUDED.draws, rating = EXCLUDED.rating;

